course: dictionary
lesson: 12268
title: The word continual
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Continual is a word for things that keep happening again and again. Read this: «The continual ringing of the bell made everyone cover their ears.» The bell did not ring once. It rang over and over, with short gaps in between, and that is what continual tells you.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. «Continual» with «made everyone cover their ears» tells you the ringing was annoying. When you meet continual, ask: does this thing keep coming back? If yes, continual fits. Then swap in your meaning and check that the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Careful writers use two similar words. Continual means repeating again and again. Continuous means going on all the time without a stop, like a continuous line. «Continual rain» has dry spells in between, but «continuous rain» never stops at all.
fact: Continual means happening again and again, with short breaks in between.
fact: «Continual» often sounds annoying, as in «continual complaints».
fact: Continual can also mean going on without stopping, but continuous is the better word for that.
fact: «Continuous» means going on without a break, like a continuous line.
fact: A good check is to swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
exercise: «The continual ringing of the bell made everyone cover their ears.» What does continual mean here? | Brand new | *Happening again and again | Happening only once, at noon | Very quiet
exercise: «Our class had continual rain all week, with sunny breaks in between.» Which word tells you the rain kept coming back? | *continual | sunny | class | week
exercise: Ben says continual means never stopping at all. Why might a science writer choose continuous instead? | It is a shorter word | Continuous means happening again and again with gaps, the same as continual | Continuous means quiet and gentle | *Continuous means going on without a stop, which is the better word for never stopping
test: Mia hears the same tap drip, drip, drip, with gaps between. Which word fits best? | *continual | empty | quiet | happy
test: Which phrase shows a worry that keeps coming back? | a sudden silence | a bright worry | a worry-free day | *a continual worry
exam: In «a continual worry», what does continual tell the reader about the worry? | It makes the child laugh | *It keeps coming back again and again | It is small and soon forgotten | It has never been felt before
